.TH qsh-tunnel-open 1  "open 0.2.0" 
.SH NAME
qsh\-tunnel\-open \- Open a tunnel to a pinned host and hold it open
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBqsh tunnel open\fR [\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-L\fR|\fB\-\-local\fR] [\fB\-\-jsonl\fR] [\fB\-R\fR|\fB\-\-remote\fR] [\fB\-D\fR|\fB\-\-dynamic\fR] [\fB\-v\fR|\fB\-\-verbose\fR]... [\fB\-q\fR|\fB\-\-quiet\fR] [\fB\-\-wait\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] [\fB\-V\fR|\fB\-\-version\fR] <\fIHOST\fR> 
.SH DESCRIPTION
Open a tunnel to a pinned host and hold it open.
.PP
This is a *value* operation that then blocks: the `Tunnel` envelope is emitted once, and the tunnel lives for as long as this foreground process does (`docs/CLI.md` §6.14). Ctrl\-C ends it.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
Emit a single `qsh.cli/v1` JSON envelope on stdout instead of human\-readable text
.TP
\fB\-L\fR, \fB\-\-local\fR \fI<SPEC>\fR
Local forward `[bind:]listen_port:host:host_port` — same grammar as the interactive `\-L` (`docs/CLI.md` §6.9). Exactly one of `\-\-local`/`\-\-remote`/`\-\-dynamic` is required
.TP
\fB\-\-jsonl\fR
Emit newline\-delimited JSON on stdout (reserved for streaming commands; non\-streaming commands emit a single line, same as `\-\-json`)
.TP
\fB\-R\fR, \fB\-\-remote\fR \fI<SPEC>\fR
Remote forward `[bind:]rport:host:hport` — same grammar as the interactive `\-R` (`docs/CLI.md` §6.9). Exactly one of `\-\-local`/`\-\-remote`/`\-\-dynamic` is required
.TP
\fB\-D\fR, \fB\-\-dynamic\fR \fI<SPEC>\fR
SOCKS5 dynamic forwarding `[bind:]port`: open a SOCKS5 proxy on this machine and, for every CONNECT a SOCKS client sends it, forward to whatever destination that CONNECT names, as seen from the peer (ADR\-0019). Exactly one of `\-\-local`/`\-\-remote`/`\-\-dynamic` is required, and `\-\-dynamic` conflicts with the other two — a single `tunnel open` call opens exactly one listener.

Still `Vec<String>`/`ArgAction::Append` so the value can be given more than once on the command line, but only ever one listener is meant to result: giving `\-\-dynamic` twice is refused with `INVALID_ARGUMENT` ("one listener per `tunnel open`"), not silently collapsed to the first or last value.

There is no ACL check at open time: each CONNECT is authorized on the peer as `forward.local`, the same action `\-\-local` uses. `DYNAMIC_FORWARD_ACL_NOTE` — "`\-D` runs SOCKS5 on this machine and authorizes every CONNECT on the peer as `forward.local`; `forward.socks` is never consulted." Works over both forward and reverse routes (ADR\-0020 decisions 1–3) — a reverse route relays each CONNECT through this machine\*(Aqs resident `qsh listen` daemon, with the same host\-local address filter a forward route enforces. The one refusal left is `UNSUPPORTED` before anything binds when the connected route never negotiated `dial\-filter.v1`; on a reverse route the message names both possible causes (the target\*(Aqs qsh may predate the capability, or this machine\*(Aqs own `qsh listen` daemon may have started before this machine\*(Aqs own upgrade) (`docs/CLI.md` §6.9).
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
Increase diagnostic verbosity on stderr (`\-v` info, `\-vv` debug, `\-vvv` trace). Never affects stdout (`docs/CLI.md` §2.2)
.TP
\fB\-q\fR, \fB\-\-quiet\fR
Suppress diagnostic output on stderr
.TP
\fB\-\-wait\fR \fI<MS>\fR [default: 0]
Milliseconds to keep retrying the initial route resolution while it answers the reverse\-registration\-stale branch. `0` (the default) is one attempt, unchanged from before this flag existed. Applies to `\-\-local`/`\-\-remote` only — combining it with `\-\-dynamic` is a clap usage error. Bound `0..=600000`; above it is `INVALID_ARGUMENT`. Full semantics: `docs/CLI.md` §6.9
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see a summary with \*(Aq\-h\*(Aq)
.TP
\fB\-V\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR
Print version
.TP
<\fIHOST\fR>
Host alias from the trust store (`qsh trust list`)
.SH VERSION
v0.2.0
